# Dog, the live loop (the button's startup script): a little Australian Shepherd on the Mac's screen that chases
# the pointer.
#
# The tap (the button's command) writes the second the dog goes home by itself to $dir/until, or removes the file
# to call the dog home now. At rest this loop only looks at that file four times a second (a builtin read, no
# process started). While the file says the dog is out, the loop keeps the overlay running: one osascript (JXA)
# process that draws the dog over every display (its own notes are below). The loop keeps $dir/out while it wants
# the dog; once that file is gone the dog runs off the screen and the overlay quits. The overlay also quits when
# this loop is gone, even killed (its parent changes), and this loop ends it at once on TERM, INT and HUP: the
# agent stops startup scripts when the phone disconnects and when it quits.
#
# The button keeps its own face (a dog, "Dog") at rest; while the dog is out, the same dog in red. That is two
# posts a visit, each one curl call that runs beside this loop (a slow agent never holds it up); nothing is posted
# while nothing changes.
#
# The dog itself is drawn as text right below (dog_palette, dog_pictures): change a colour or a pixel there. What
# it does and how fast is at the top of the overlay (dog_js). To look at the result without the phone:
#   zsh startup.zsh --sheet dog.png  every picture facing right and left, magnified, into dog.png (no window)
#   zsh startup.zsh --sim            the dog chasing a made-up pointer over two made-up displays, a line per change
#   DOG_DRY=1 zsh startup.zsh        the loop as the agent runs it, but posts to the button are printed, not sent;
#                                    with DOG_GHOST=1 too the dog is not drawn either: what it does is printed.
#                                    Let it out with tap.zsh and the same DESMAPTIC_STORAGE.
emulate -R zsh                                  # zsh's own options, whatever ~/.zshenv set
export LC_ALL=C
export PATH="/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin"

# ---- The dog --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A picture is 80 pixels wide and 60 high, one letter per pixel; "." is see-through. The dog stands on the bottom
# row and faces right: the dog facing left is the mirror image. Every picture starts with "= name", and the names
# are what the overlay asks for (sit, blink, wag1-2, pant1-2, yawn, tilt, scratch1-2, lie, lie2-3, lieblink,
# sleep1-2, alert, jump, bow, run1-3, and z: the letter that floats up from a sleeping dog), so keep them. Every
# dog picture is the size of sit; z may be any size. A letter needs a colour in the palette: the letter, then red
# green blue in hex. One pixel is 1 pt on the screen (PT in dog_js): 2 x 2 pixels of a Retina display, kept sharp.
# Light comes from the top left: most colours have a lit, a plain and a shaded tone.
# The coat is a blue merle. For a red merle: l E2CABA, g C49A84, d 98725F, x 6E4E40 (the coat), b 7E4530, a 9A5E44
# (the patches, liver), and k 2A1A14 (the outline and the nose: a red merle's nose is liver); i E0A050 for two
# amber eyes. For a black tri: l 474C56, g 2F333B, d 23262D, x 1C1E24, b 23262D, a 3A3F49 — the white and the
# copper stay as they are.
dog_palette='
k  1A1C22   outline, nose, pupils
x  4C5462   the deepest grey: folds between the parts of the coat
d  707B8B   the coat in shadow
g  9CA7B6   merle grey: the coat
l  C8D0DB   the coat in the light
b  2B2F38   the black patches of the merle
a  4D5564   the black patches in the light and along the outline, the shine on the nose
w  F7F6F1   white: blaze, muzzle, ruff, chest, legs, feet; the z of a sleeping dog
v  D3D7DE   white in shadow, the far legs
u  A6ADB8   white in deep shadow: folds, toes, the far legs of the running dog
o  E5904C   copper in the light
t  C2642B   copper: cheeks, eyebrows, legs
c  8A4220   copper in shadow
e  A8652A   the brown eye
i  62CBF5   the blue eye
s  FFFFFF   the shine in the eyes
p  F27A8E   tongue
q  C44D68   tongue in shadow
r  6E2030   inside the mouth
'

